WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
All right, so welcome to week five module one.

2
00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.640
Now this module is going to cover the first phase of delivery.

3
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.200
So if you think about the bit framework, the first phase is all about build,

4
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.880
building the MVP and using modern AI tools to build quickly,

5
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.880
show a demo, get feedback from your client and also validate before going

6
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:17.320
deeper.

7
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.400
And at this stage, you may have already have a deal closed.

8
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.680
Now it's time for you to actually deliver.

9
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.760
And this is where the actual work starts.

10
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.880
Remember, when we talked about the bit framework,

11
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.840
it's all about having a phase four building, then integrating and then tuning,

12
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.360
which we broadly discussed in the previous week.

13
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.280
Now, the question is, how do you actually do it?

14
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.000
First, we need to start with build.

15
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.040
And the build phase is all about creating the MVP, the minimum viable product.

16
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.720
And our goal in this case is to get something working as quickly as possible.

17
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.160
Not perfect, but working,

18
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.640
something that you can demo to your client in your next client call with them

19
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.240
so that you can get feedback.

20
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.880
And the reason why speed matters in this case is because you need to validate,

21
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.160
right, you need to show the client something real so that they can give bills

22
00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.560
for you before you go too deep.

23
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.560
Because the last thing you want is for you to get it to 100% completion

24
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.040
without you involving your client in the journey.

25
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.080
And often in that case, from what I've seen,

26
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.480
it doesn't really have to spec with what the client wants.

27
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.160
Because remember, with any engagement,

28
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.040
most likely, especially for non-defined system outputs like this AI agent space

29
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.080
that is continuous developing, even the client doesn't really know

30
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.520
exactly what they're looking for.

31
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.320
And so what's important in this phase is that you actually involve them

32
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.520
in the journey and show them your output as you go,

33
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.640
so that you can signify that working relationship and collaboration with your

34
00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.080
client.

35
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.320
And what I highly recommend for you to start with,

36
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.880
no matter what system or what AI agent you're trying to build for the client,

37
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.280
is for you to start with the tools available today.

38
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.400
And one of the simplest and best ways to build this is to open up an agenda

39
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:42.960
platform,

40
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.880
much like the ones that we teach in the technical courses here in our community

41
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:46.880
,

42
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.320
like anti-gravity or cloud code, and literally give it your proposal,

43
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.720
which means that PDF that you send to your client,

44
00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.070
if you just feed that to cloud code or anti-gravity and have it walk you

45
00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:56.320
through

46
00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.200
what could be your approaches into achieving that specific build that you want.

47
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.040
And this is great because with any client that you're working with,

48
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.920
there may be differences in spec and differences in the output that is required

49
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:07.360
from you.

50
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.240
But what is common among them is if you use AI models

51
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.760
who have a wealth of knowledge among all the automation tools and stacks

52
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.000
that is available right now and is also constantly being updated,

53
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.320
then what will happen is that you will have the latest and best ways

54
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.840
to tackle that specific problem.

55
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.440
So that's why it's important to let AI guide you in this case,

56
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.920
have it guide you through what's needed and it'll help you break down the

57
00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:27.560
requirements,

58
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.640
suggest an approach and also start building.

59
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.080
And also as a reminder, what's important is you build the core functionality

60
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:33.440
first.

61
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.720
So whatever you're building, whether it's an agent, an automation or a system,

62
00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.200
you need to ask, what's the one thing that this needs to do?

63
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.840
And you build that first and work backwards from it because simplicity is

64
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:43.600
always your friend.

65
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.680
If you try to add bells and whistles at the get-go without first building

66
00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.600
that solution that would output 80% of the intended impact for your client,

67
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.600
then it's very possible that whatever you're building

68
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.800
would not fit the client's requirements in the end.

69
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.400
And to give a more concrete example,

70
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.200
this is a pipeline and automation system that we're building with a partner.

71
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.440
And what we're basically doing here is finding leads in the Texas area,

72
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.440
mostly in the salon and spa space.

73
00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.260
But what's common amongst these companies is that their websites are not

74
00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.040
optimized.

75
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.480
This is one example of a lead that we extracted and this is their website.

76
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.680
It's OK, but it's quite obvious how much you can actually optimize this.

77
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.360
And so as part of that automation pipeline is we basically use Geminitary.1

78
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.960
Pro in order to improve these websites programmatically.

79
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.240
So this is just one example of the improved one where you can see there's

80
00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.880
better interactability within the website and it's much more refined as well.

81
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.680
And we basically did that for all of these leads.

82
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.000
Now, this whole pipeline, it was built in anti-gravity.

83
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.840
And you can see here, if you're just thinking about the core functionality

84
00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.200
or the minimum viable product, what you need to be focused on is coming up

85
00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.360
with some sort of graphic like this, which lets you clearly illustrate and

86
00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:45.720
explain

87
00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.360
to the client what it is that you're trying to do, which is the goal here.

88
00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.440
And what is your process in doing it?

89
00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.680
At least for this case, we show that we are calling the web to generate leads

90
00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:54.600
for us automatically.

91
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.920
We call on Geminitary.1 Pro to redesign that website for us.

92
00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.240
We deploy it to Vercel, which is where we host that website all for free.

93
00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.230
And then we send an automated called email sequence with a live link to their

94
00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:04.680
new site,

95
00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.880
which is important because the partner we're working with,

96
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.480
they're a website redesign firm.

97
00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.920
And this sort of flow is very important for them to get new clients

98
00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:12.640
and leads as well.

99
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.360
But if you're not focused on the core functionality,

100
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.810
then you may start to add in futures to this whole pipeline like extending

101
00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:20.080
beyond

102
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.200
the Texas area, for example, and maybe apart from redesigning like one page as

103
00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:24.720
a sample,

104
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.400
you try and build a whole website already for your leads,

105
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.440
which at this point, since you're just pitching them, it's not yet important.

106
00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.400
Or maybe you try to deploy it in actual.com websites,

107
00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:36.720
which is obviously not the trick here, because what we're doing is simply using

108
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.840
Vercel's free program that allows us to deploy to the web instantly.

109
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.880
And so if you think about it, there's so many ways

110
00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.760
that you can try and add more bells and whistles to this pipeline.

111
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.200
But if you are working for speed and you're just thinking about the minimum

112
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.600
viable product, you just need to illustrate that this sort of pipeline can be

113
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:51.840
done

114
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.880
and you need to demo it to your client, just like what I did to you right now

115
00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.560
on showing the before and after look of the websites.

116
00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.240
And that is what's most important in the build phase.

117
00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.720
And like I mentioned, before you do any sort of integration with their system,

118
00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:07.600
you first need to do a demo with them and how to do a demo is similar to what

119
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.960
we just did, where it's very simple.

120
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.400
You just share your screen, you walk them through what it does

121
00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.180
and have like a visual like I shared earlier that goes through exactly what you

122
00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:15.360
did

123
00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.440
and exactly what this AI agent does.

124
00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.240
And this way you achieve three things.

125
00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:23.360
One is you get early feedback so they can give you builds before you invest too

126
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:23.600
much

127
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.840
time and it's much better to learn something's wrong now than after full

128
00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:27.480
integration.

129
00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.600
The second thing is that they feel involved.

130
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.200
So especially for complex systems based projects.

131
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.000
The client needs to feel like they're part of the journey, like I said earlier.

132
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.520
If you just hand them like a turnkey solution at the end of the week, for

133
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.040
example,

134
00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.240
they won't feel like they got their money's worth.

135
00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:45.640
It's just the nature of our business that if a client feels like you achieve

136
00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.720
a solution too easily, they feel like it's not actually worth their money.

137
00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:52.680
And it's the same reason why if you think about books, one of the core reasons

138
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:56.000
why there's a lot of books that could have been an article, for example,

139
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.560
because they just have like one core idea and they just sprinkle anecdotes

140
00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.840
to fill in like a 200 to 300 page book.

141
00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.080
The reason why is because if you just have one simple idea

142
00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:09.040
and you have a really thin book, then you probably won't pay $50 for that book.

143
00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.960
But if it's the same simple idea, repeated to anecdotes

144
00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.520
in order to fill in like a 200 to 300 page book,

145
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.360
then people are more willing to pay $50 for that because they feel like they're

146
00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.320
getting their money's worth.

147
00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:21.240
And so that's like a rule for publishers.

148
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:23.920
And it's also part of the reason why there's so many books.

149
00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.280
But when you read it, it's just a lot of anecdotes and a lot of examples

150
00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.920
coming from that simple, but still valid idea.

151
00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:31.160
So it's the same thing is the point.

152
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:33.840
Even if the solution is simple, you don't necessarily want to give them

153
00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.840
the completed product and say, OK, that's done, engagement over.

154
00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.840
You actually need to involve them in the journey and do a demo

155
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.640
in order to get feedback as you go.

156
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.440
So there's always a psychological element to every engagement

157
00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:45.360
that you are working with.

158
00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.360
And last but not least is clarity.

159
00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.840
So again, like I mentioned, most clients don't know exactly what they want

160
00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:54.880
until they see something, especially in our space where this AI agents

161
00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.600
as a service is still emerging, most likely, whoever you're talking to,

162
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:02.440
especially businesses who are not that tech savvy or not as AI involved.

163
00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:06.120
Most likely the only AI that they have seen and known is Chi Chi BT.

164
00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.200
And so doing a demo helps clarify the intention for them.

165
00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.400
And when you do the demo, there are some questions that you can ask.

166
00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.160
Does this solve the problem we discussed?

167
00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.440
Is there anything missing?

168
00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:16.880
What would make this more useful?

169
00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.280
Those are just some three questions that you can ask in order

170
00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.160
to stimulate conversation with your client.

171
00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:24.080
And you can also capture these notes in order to improve the solution.

172
00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:27.400
And also goes without saying and similar to what we mentioned in previous

173
00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.080
modules, you need to document everything.

174
00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:33.320
Take notes, record the call, use tools like Fathom or Fireflies that AI

175
00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:35.480
so that you can generate those notes automatically.

176
00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.880
And also, I'm a big proponent of simplicity here, as you may have noticed.

177
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.440
And I always believe that simple beats complex.

178
00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:43.560
If you don't know how to explain something simply,

179
00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.960
then that probably means that you just don't understand it as deeply as you

180
00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:47.400
think.

181
00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.040
And this is also true when it comes to client conversations,

182
00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:54.160
where simple solutions almost always are better appreciated than complex ones.

183
00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.640
Because if your client has a bit of an understanding around what it is

184
00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.160
that you are building for them, then they will like you more that will

185
00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.600
foster the relationship with them and you can get more projects with them

186
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:04.960
and their network as well.

187
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.680
And this is important because maybe from your background,

188
00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.840
since you may have a technical background yourself,

189
00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.000
you may have the tendency for over-engineering, right?

190
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.470
Which is just natural, especially if you're in this field where you are working

191
00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:16.360
in AI.

192
00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:19.200
So there's always that temptation to add features to make it impressive.

193
00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.520
But honestly, since we're in the MVP phase,

194
00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:23.840
it's always just better to ship it and show it.

195
00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:27.480
And always, you can just iterate later and just add more and improve it as you

196
00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:27.840
go.

197
00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.580
But what you need to understand is you need to get the core functionality right

198
00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:32.080
first.

199
00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:34.600
The last thing that I want to mention here, which is very important,

200
00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:37.880
is this concept of under-promising and over-delivering.

201
00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:40.720
So remember that this is a game of expectation.

202
00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:45.070
If you told them that your delivery will take two weeks and you deliver in one

203
00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:45.160
week,

204
00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:46.840
then that builds your reputation, right?

205
00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.800
Gives you leverage for future work and it strengthens the relationship.

206
00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.680
This is why anchoring is so important,

207
00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:53.920
which is part of what we talked about in the pricing.

208
00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:57.850
And this is also why if you were to choose whether to under-commit or over-

209
00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:58.200
commit,

210
00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.360
the obvious answer is always to under-commit.

211
00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:03.200
Because you can always exceed that when it comes to delivering the output.

212
00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.640
So if you think that you can get 100 customers for your client, for example,

213
00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:09.480
then let's say you can commit to 70 customers, for example,

214
00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.080
if you think the client will say yes to that.

215
00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.520
And then once you deliver, you can just go and provide the 100 customers anyway

216
00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:14.560
,

217
00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:17.760
and that over-delivery will be remembered by your client, for sure.

218
00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:21.200
So there, just to summarize, create the MVP using agentic tools.

219
00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:24.520
I think starting with those is one of the strongest leverage pieces

220
00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:25.560
that you can do right now.

221
00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:29.360
Show a demo, get feedback, document everything and keep it simple.

222
00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.320
In the next module, we'll go to the integrate phase,

223
00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:34.350
which is basically getting your solution that is now living in your staging

224
00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:34.960
environment,

225
00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.320
that you just demoed to your client, working in their systems.
